CAT = "caautoDriveline"; SEG = "DRV"
# RSOS 2023 MWA D: Diagnoses and repairs driveline systems (D-12, D-13)
Q = [
("Engine speed climbs but road speed does not when accelerating hard in top gear. What is the likely fault?",
 "A slipping clutch", ["A dragging clutch", "A worn synchronizer", "A noisy release bearing"],
 "A slipping clutch lets the engine run faster than the input shaft under high torque. Oil on the disc, a worn disc or no pedal free play are common causes."),
("A manual transmission grinds going into any gear from a stop. What is a likely cause?",
 "A dragging clutch", ["A slipping clutch", "Low gear oil level", "A worn input shaft seal"],
 "If the clutch does not fully release, the input shaft keeps turning and every gear grinds. Air in the hydraulic system or excess free play are common causes."),
("A grinding noise happens only when shifting into third gear. What is the likely cause?",
 "The third gear synchro", ["A dragging clutch", "A bad release bearing", "A worn pilot bearing"],
 "A fault that affects one gear points to that gear's synchronizer, which matches shaft and gear speeds before engagement. A dragging clutch would affect every gear."),
("A noise is heard only while the clutch pedal is pressed down. Which part is likely?",
 "The release bearing", ["The input shaft bearing", "The countershaft bearing", "The flywheel ring gear"],
 "The release bearing only spins under load when the pedal is pressed. An input shaft bearing is noisy with the pedal up in neutral and quiet when it is pressed."),
("With the engine idling in neutral, a noise goes away when the clutch pedal is pressed. What is likely worn?",
 "The input shaft bearing", ["The release bearing", "The pilot bearing", "The clutch master cylinder"],
 "Pressing the pedal stops the input shaft from turning, so a noise that stops is coming from a part turned by it. The release bearing would be loudest with the pedal down."),
("What is the job of a dual-mass flywheel?",
 "To absorb driveline vibration", ["To raise engine compression", "To replace the clutch disc", "To drive the oil pump"],
 "Springs between the two masses damp engine torsional vibration before it reaches the transmission. Excess free play or rattle between the masses means replacement."),
("In a simple planetary gearset, what happens when any two members are locked together?",
 "Direct drive", ["Reverse rotation", "Maximum reduction", "Overdrive ratio"],
 "Locking any two members locks the whole set, so it turns as one unit. Holding the carrier gives reverse."),
("In a simple planetary gearset, which member is held to produce reverse?",
 "The planet carrier", ["The sun gear", "The ring gear", "The sun and ring together"],
 "With the carrier held, the planets act as idlers and the ring turns opposite to the sun. That reverses direction."),
("A ring gear has 41 teeth and the pinion has 11. What is the axle ratio?",
 "3.73:1", ["3.41:1", "4.10:1", "2.68:1"],
 "Divide ring gear teeth by pinion teeth: 41 / 11 = 3.727, written as 3.73:1."),
("What does a torque converter clutch do when it applies?",
 "Locks turbine to cover", ["Holds the stator still", "Multiplies engine torque", "Drives the fluid pump"],
 "Lock-up mechanically connects the turbine to the converter cover, removing fluid slip at cruise. That cuts heat and improves fuel economy."),
("A stall test gives an rpm well above specification. What does this indicate?",
 "Slipping clutches or bands", ["Low engine power output", "A seized stator one-way clutch", "A restricted exhaust system"],
 "If the holding devices slip, the engine can spin faster than the converter's design stall speed. A low reading points to engine power or the stator clutch."),
("A vehicle stalls when shifted into drive at a stop. What is a likely cause in the transmission?",
 "TCC stuck applied", ["Low line pressure", "A worn forward band", "A slipping sprag clutch"],
 "If the converter clutch stays locked, the engine is tied directly to the wheels and stalls when they stop. A stuck TCC solenoid or valve is a common cause."),
("How is the fluid level checked on many sealed automatic transmissions?",
 "At a plug, at set temperature", ["With a dipstick, engine cold", "By weighing the drained fluid", "By filling to the cooler line"],
 "Sealed units have no dipstick. The fluid is checked at a level plug with the engine running and the fluid at the temperature the maker gives, usually read with a scan tool."),
("Why must a CVT use the fluid its maker specifies?",
 "Belt grip relies on it", ["It cools the engine as well", "It is the same as gear oil", "It never needs changing"],
 "A CVT transfers power through friction between the belt or chain and the pulleys. The wrong fluid changes that friction and can cause slipping and damage."),
("A clicking noise comes from a front-drive axle only during sharp turns. What is likely worn?",
 "The outer CV joint", ["The inner CV joint", "The wheel bearing", "The differential side gear"],
 "The outer joint works at its steepest angles during tight turns, so wear shows as clicking there. Inner joint wear usually shows as shudder on acceleration."),
("A front-drive vehicle shudders on hard acceleration from a stop. Which part is a likely cause?",
 "A worn inner CV joint", ["A worn outer CV joint", "A loose wheel bearing", "A bent steering knuckle"],
 "Inner tripod joints plunge in and out, and wear there causes a shudder under load. Outer joint wear is usually heard as clicking in turns."),
("What is the most common cause of CV joint failure?",
 "A torn boot", ["Low transmission fluid", "Wrong tire pressure", "High engine speed"],
 "A split boot lets the grease out and dirt and water in, which wears the joint quickly. Checking boots at every service catches this early."),
("For a two-joint driveshaft to run smoothly, how should the U-joint working angles be set?",
 "Equal and opposite", ["Both at zero degrees", "One at twice the other", "As steep as possible"],
 "A U-joint speeds up and slows down twice per turn at an angle. With equal and opposite angles and yokes in phase, the second joint cancels the first."),
("A driveline vibration changes with road speed but not with engine speed. Where is it likely?",
 "After the transmission", ["In the engine mounts", "In the flywheel", "In the torque converter"],
 "Parts that turn with road speed, such as the driveshaft, axles, tires and wheels, cause speed-related vibration. Engine-related vibration follows rpm in any gear."),
("A wheel bearing growls louder when the vehicle turns left. Which bearing is most likely worn?",
 "The right side bearing", ["The left side bearing", "The pinion bearing", "The carrier bearing"],
 "Turning left shifts weight onto the right side wheels and loads their bearings more. A noise that grows in left turns usually points to the right side."),
("Which adjustment mainly moves the gear tooth contact pattern toward heel or toe?",
 "Backlash", ["Pinion depth", "Pinion bearing preload", "Axle shaft end play"],
 "Moving the ring gear toward or away from the pinion changes backlash and slides the pattern along the tooth from heel to toe. Pinion depth moves it between face and flank."),
("A pinion nut on a crush sleeve design is tightened past preload spec. What should be done?",
 "Replace the crush sleeve", ["Back the nut off to spec", "Add a shim behind the nut", "Leave it if it turns freely"],
 "An over-crushed sleeve no longer holds the right preload when loosened. The usual fix is a new sleeve and nut, then tightening slowly to the preload spec."),
("A clutch-type limited-slip differential chatters in tight turns. What is a common fix?",
 "Add friction modifier", ["Use thinner gear oil only", "Tighten the pinion nut", "Reduce the backlash"],
 "The clutch packs need a friction modifier in the gear oil to slip smoothly. Without enough of it, the plates stick and release, causing chatter."),
("Why should a part-time 4WD system not be used in 4WD on dry pavement?",
 "It causes driveline windup", ["It overheats the engine", "It drains the battery", "It wears the brakes out"],
 "With no centre differential, the front and rear axles are locked together. On dry pavement they cannot turn at different speeds in turns, so stress builds in the driveline."),
("Why must all four tires on many AWD vehicles be the same size and wear?",
 "To protect the centre coupling", ["To keep the tire warranty valid", "To reduce tire rolling noise", "To keep the speedometer exact"],
 "Mismatched diameters make the axles turn at different speeds all the time. The centre coupling or transfer case slips constantly and can overheat or fail."),
("With an open differential, what happens when one drive wheel is on ice?",
 "The wheel on ice spins", ["Both wheels lock together", "The gripping wheel gets full torque", "The driveshaft disengages"],
 "An open differential applies equal torque to both wheels, limited by the wheel with least grip. The wheel on ice spins and the other gets almost no drive."),
("A whine from the differential that is loudest under acceleration usually points to what?",
 "Gear wear or pinion setup", ["A worn axle shaft seal", "A loose driveshaft bolt", "A dry rear U-joint bearing"],
 "Gear noise that changes on drive and coast comes from the ring and pinion contact. Wear or a wrong pinion depth or backlash is the usual cause."),
("What does a hydraulic clutch slave cylinder do?",
 "Moves the release fork", ["Stores clutch fluid", "Damps flywheel vibration", "Holds the pressure plate"],
 "Pedal force builds pressure in the master cylinder, and the slave cylinder turns it into movement at the release fork or a concentric release bearing."),
]
